7.25,0,35.51,69.16,0,31.68,70.33,0,31.1,68.42,6,34.92,67.25,6,35.51,69.16,6,31.68,70.33,6],"f":[0,2,1,0,3,2,4,5,6,4,6,7,0,1,5,0,5,4,1,2,6,1,6,5,2,3,7,2,7,6,3,0,4,3,4,7]},"m":"white"}],"notes":"Art Gallery of Ontario, Toronto, after Frank Gehry's Transformation AGO (2008): on Dundas Street the Galleria Italia, a 138 m bulging facade of glass on Douglas-fir ribs that swells out over the street and lifts at its ends; the pale older wings behind, Walker Court's skylight and the gallery skylights; over the south, the blue titanium-clad tower of contemporary galleries with its glass stair; the Grange (1817), the Georgian brick house, in Grange Park behind. Turned 17 degrees to Toronto's street grid as the ground file shows. Written from general knowledge; guessed: the older wings' heights, the tower's size (about 40 by 32 m, 43 m high), the Galleria's exact curve. The Galleria's swelling is built from slices; the tower's titanium is drawn in the blue 'water' colour. No photographs were available. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the ground file's OpenStreetMap outlines, not photographs (none reachable): the whole museum stood about 22 m north-west of its outline (the five-storey block between Dundas, McCaul and Beverley), so it was moved 22 m east and 22 m south onto it (on 65% before, 85% now); the blue tower was drawn back 15 m north so it rises from the block's south part rather than past its edge, and the Grange moved to the small building the outlines show against the gallery's south side, the tower behind it as seen from the park; the site widened to 200 m (1.9 m voxels) to keep Grange Park and its trees in view. Still guessed: the older wings' heights and the tower's size and exact place."}